WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.152
Safe path construction starts from a trusted base directory or storage
root chosen by the application, not from a complete path supplied by a
user.

2
00:00:08.152 --> 00:00:15.243
The application should combine trusted configuration with controlled
identifiers or approved names using path-aware APIs.

3
00:00:15.243 --> 00:00:19.153
String concatenation is fragile because path values have structure.

4
00:00:19.153 --> 00:00:28.565
Separators, relative segments, absolute forms, drive or root indicators,
encoding differences, and platform behavior can all change how a final
path is interpreted.

5
00:00:28.565 --> 00:00:35.325
Canonicalization is the process of resolving a path into a consistent form
so it can be compared or checked more reliably.

6
00:00:35.325 --> 00:00:41.753
At a high level, teams often normalize a path, resolve relative meaning,
and compare the result to an approved base.

7
00:00:41.753 --> 00:00:46.923
This can help identify whether the final path remains inside the intended
storage location.

8
00:00:46.923 --> 00:00:50.833
Canonicalization is useful, but it is not a complete design by itself.

9
00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:55.141
It must be paired with clear storage policy, allowlists, and
authorization.

10
00:00:55.141 --> 00:01:05.811
A base-directory containment check asks a simple question: after the path
is resolved according to the platform and storage model, is the final
location still inside the approved directory?

11
00:01:05.811 --> 00:01:14.029
That check should happen before sensitive reads or writes, and the code
should handle missing files, invalid names, and permission errors safely.

12
00:01:14.029 --> 00:01:19.464
A safe failure should not expose internal paths or automatically fall back
to a broader location.

13
00:01:19.464 --> 00:01:24.567
Path APIs are preferable to ad hoc text manipulation because they
represent paths as paths.

14
00:01:24.567 --> 00:01:26.224
They still require judgment.

15
00:01:26.224 --> 00:01:37.756
Developers must understand whether links are followed, when resolution
touches the filesystem, how errors are handled, and whether a later
operation can target something different from what was checked.

16
00:01:37.756 --> 00:01:46.040
Safe path construction is a pattern: trusted base, controlled input, path-
aware joining, resolved containment, authorization, and careful use.
